    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d408">

                <pb facs="0711" n="697" sameAs="frus:frus1944v01/pg697"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.CC/8–144</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The British Chargé</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Campbell</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 473</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-08-01">August 1, 1944</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honour to inform you under
                    instructions from His Majesty’s Principal Secretary of State for Foreign
                    Affairs, that The Honourable Sir Alexander Cadogan, G.C.M.G., K.C.B., Permanent
                    Under Secretary of State for Foreign Affairs, has been appointed to lead the
                    United Kingdom Delegation to the Conference at Dumbarton Oaks.</p>
                <p>The principal members of the Delegation in addition to Sir Alexander Cadogan will
                    be: <list>
                        <item>Sir W. Malkin,<anchor n="52" xml:id="d408-f1"/> G.C.M.G., C.B., K.C.
                            (the Legal Adviser, Foreign Office);</item>
                        <item>Admiral Sir Percy Noble,<anchor n="53" xml:id="d408-f2"/>
                        </item>
                        <item>Lieutenant General G. N. Macready,<anchor n="54" xml:id="d408-f3"/>
                        </item>
                        <item>Air Marshal Sir William Welch,<anchor n="55" xml:id="d408-f4"/>
                        </item>
                        <item>Mr. Gladwyn Jebb, C.M.G., (Foreign Office Secretary-General)<anchor
                                n="56" xml:id="d408-f5"/>
                        </item>
                        <item>Mr. A. H. Poynton, (Colonial Office)</item>
                        <item>Colonel Capel Dunn, (War Cabinet Offices)<anchor n="57"
                                xml:id="d408-f6"/>
                        </item>
                        <item>Mr. Peter Loxley, (Private Secretary to Sir A. Cadogan)</item>
                        <item>Professor Webster, (Foreign Office)<anchor n="58" xml:id="d408-f7"/>
                        </item>
                        <item>Mr. Paul Falla, (Foreign Office).<anchor n="59" xml:id="d408-f8"/>
                        </item>
                    </list>
                </p>
                <p>The secretariat accompanying the Delegation will be composed of Miss
                    Cole-Hamilton, Miss Thomas and Miss Collard.</p>
                <pb facs="0712" n="698" xml:id="pg698"/>
                <p>It is expected that the Delegation will arrive in the United States, by sea, at
                    New York on or about August 11th. Any further particulars which may be received
                    about the Delegation will be communicated to the Department as soon as they are
                    received. It is possible that additions may still be made to the personnel of
                    the Delegation.<anchor n="60" xml:id="d408-f9"/>
                </p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Ronald</hi> I. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Campbell</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d408-f1" n="52" xml:id="d408-f1-content">
                <p>Sir William Malkin.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d408-f2" n="53" xml:id="d408-f2-content">
                <p>Head of British Naval Delegation in Washington since 1942.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d408-f3" n="54" xml:id="d408-f3-content">
                <p>Maj. Gen. Gordon N. Macready, Chief of British Army Staff in Washington since
                    1942; his assistant was Maj. Gen. M. F. Grove-White.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d408-f4" n="55" xml:id="d408-f4-content">
                <p>Head of Royal Air Force Delegation in Washington since 1943.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d408-f5" n="56" xml:id="d408-f5-content">
                <p>Gladwyn Jebb, Councillor, Foreign Office, Secretary General of the British
                    Delegation.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d408-f6" n="57" xml:id="d408-f6-content">
                <p>Military Assistant Secretary of the War Cabinet.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d408-f7" n="58" xml:id="d408-f7-content">
                <p>Charles K. Webster, Research Department of the Foreign Office; professor of
                    international history, London School of Economics since 1932.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d408-f8" n="59" xml:id="d408-f8-content">
                <p>Economic and Reconstruction Department of the Foreign Office.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d408-f9" n="60" xml:id="d408-f9-content">
                <p>Two additional members listed in <hi rendition="#italic">Postwar Foreign Policy
                        Preparation</hi> were: Paul Gore-Booth, First Secretary, British Embassy,
                    and A. R. K. Mackenzie, Press Officer.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
